lawmakers in germany have voted in favor  of mandatory vaccines for health care  workers the law requires medical staff  to show proof of vaccination or recovery  by mid-march exceptions will be made on  health grounds dentists pharmacists and  vets will also be allowed to give jabs  germany is struggling to break the  fourth wave of the pandemic the new  health minister who is also an  epidemiologist hopes the measures will  stop the omicron variant from having an  overwhelming impact  we want to strengthen our infection  protection law to give all german states  the tools they need to get a grip on  infection levels  they can ban large public gatherings and  big sports events  they can close down bars clubs and  discos  they'll even be able to shut restaurants  if necessary  we're providing the means to break the  delta wave regionally and nationally and  doing all we can to prevent a powerful  omicron wave  let's get more on this from our  political correspondent jared reed uh  jared talk us through what the lawmakers  have decided today  well michael lawmakers have decided that  healthcare workers in hospitals and in  elderly care homes will have to get a  covert jab by march of next year they'll  have to prove that by march or they'll  have to prove too that they have been  recovered or that they have an exemption  from this and if they don't then they  won't be able to come to work what was  debated and passed today was an update  to the infection  law that also gives other kinds of  health care workers so pharmacists  dentists and even veterinarians the  ability to be able to administer covert  jobs some more  rights were given to states as well they  this will allow them to order the  closures of clubs discos uh and  restaurants so this is all done and  dusted this has passed both of the  houses of parliament here so we have  this on one side compulsory vaccinations  in for some uh  for healthcare workers on the other hand  we know that very soon the parliament is  going to be debating compulsory vaccines  for all this is something that uh the  new chancellor olaf schultz would like  to see simply because of the uh not  enough people are vaccinated fully  vaccinated here in germany still under  70 of the population  no doubt tough measures for tough times  give our international viewers a sense  of how critical the covet situation is  in germany right now jared  well it is critical it's been named by  the new government as its number one  priority and we know that infection  numbers are still far too high they have  sort of been leveling off over the last  week or so but it is still too high we  know that there are hundreds of people  who are dying of covert 19 still in  germany every day so the government set  up a crisis team headed by a two-star  general that will report to it  every day olaf schultz has also said  that he wants uh 30 million vaccines to  be administered by christmas time it  looks like he might get there it's  looking fairly promising on thursday we  know that around a million vaccines were  administered here in germany well  despite all this vaccine hesitancy is  persistent is it likely jared that even  workers in the medical sector will  resist compulsory vaccination  well this has been quite a worry of the  government it's a bit of a tricky  balancing act because over the last two  years have been a lot of healthcare  staff that have left the profession so  the government doesn't want more to  leave but it also doesn't want  people who work in the healthcare sector  to come into contact with covert 19 and  pass it on to sick and elderly people so  it is a real juggling act we know  generally in the population there is  strong support for compulsory vaccines  as always a deep thanks jared reed  let's talk about all this with  epidemiologist tobias kurt mr kurt in  view of the infection figures do you  think government should pass these  suggested measures  yes i think so there should be tools  where the government or governmental  structures can intervene and  put measures in place that the numbers  will go down that's a really important  thing and we started unfortunately too  late to break the delta wave earlier and  now we need to get these numbers down as  quickly as possible to be prepared for  the omicron wave  do you believe the suggested measures  will be effective enough to break this  fourth wave in germany  we know since the beginning that if we  reduce the contact among people and  reduce the ability that people can  mingle in in large events sport events  etc that will bring the numbers down so  that was the case in the first wave the  second wave the third wave and now on  the fourth wave we're seeing some  numbers already indicating that the  infection rate goes down but we need to  do better to really get these numbers  down more quickly  uh the new uh health minister is khal  lauterbach an epidemiologist who many  germans trust what would you like to see  from him in the coming weeks and months  i'm in not a position to really give him  recommendation he's an expert in the  field and he needs to evaluate now very  quickly the situation the vaccine the  the next possible steps that the  government can do and really communicate  clearly to the people and make clear  that everybody as good as possible  understands the urgency that we are in  right now  i want to ask you potentially a  sensitive question but one that needs to  be asked what should the previous  government have done in order to prevent  this fourth wave from occurring the way  it  occurs to be  happening right now  i think that we learned a lot during the  pandemic in the beginning it was very  difficult to judge what could be done  and then things  actually went into place i think the the  critical point was the  the in between two governments where  people did not make decisions and really  we allowed the delta wave to increase so  it was not a  question of a specific ministry or a  specific government it was that the  decision makers did not understand the  severity of the situation and that we  should have acted earlier now everything  is in place the booster campaign for the  boost vaccination is ongoing that's  really important we need to increase  also the test capacities and communicate  to the people more clearly that whenever  they have smaller symptoms they stay  home they need to get tested and make  sure that i'm not spreading the  infection specifically while we're  waiting in germany to the omicron  variant  thank you so much that's epidemiologist  tobias kurt  thank you for having me
 

